{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-36","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"36","title":"A joint resolution providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection relating to \"Prohibition on Creditors and Consumer Reporting Agencies Concerning Medical Information (Regulation V)\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-11","latestActionDate":"2025-03-11","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Banking, Housing, and Urban Affairs.","policyArea":"Finance and Financial Sector","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"R000605","cosponsorBioguideIds":["S001184","H000601","C000880","L000571","C001056"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the final rule issued by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau titled Prohibition on Creditors and Consumer Reporting Agencies Concerning Medical Information (Regulation V) and published on January 14, 2025. This rule prohibits the inclusion of medical debt on credit reports and prohibits creditors from considering medical debt when making credit eligibility determinations.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/36","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres36.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:29.438Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-36","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would overturn a Consumer Financial Protection Bureau rule published in January 2025. That rule prohibits medical debt from appearing on consumer credit reports and bars lenders from using medical debt when deciding whether to extend credit. If this resolution passes, the CFPB rule would be nullified and the prior framework would remain in effect.","industries":[{"industry":"finance-banking","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects lenders' ability to consider medical debt in credit eligibility decisions."},{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Credit reporting agencies, which include major consumer reporting bureaus, would be affected by changes to what information they may include in reports."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Hospitals and health systems that extend credit or report unpaid medical bills would be affected by whether medical debt appears on credit reports."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"934f8a7f476d333d2c4fe064","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:51:25.842Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the final rule issued by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau titled Prohibition on Creditors and Consumer Reporting Agencies Concerning Medical Information (Regulation V) and published on January 14, 2025.","whoItTouches":"Anyone with a bank account, a loan or savings.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in dealing with banks and lenders. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-sjres-36","bioguideId":"R000605","cycle":2026,"score":0.061836715311704184,"matches":[{"industry":"finance-banking","donorAmount":283650,"donorShare":0.11163645116801423,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.04242185144384541},{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":110500,"donorShare":0.043489609920908064,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.014786467373108741},{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":42000,"donorShare":0.016529987481250123,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.004628396494750034}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence.